      "This is the pitcher most people picture when someone says water filter pitcher, and there's a reason it's sold in basically every big-box store, grocery chain, and pharmacy in the country. The BrightSpring Classic is a straightforward activated carbon filter that does a genuinely good job at what it's designed for: pulling chlorine taste and odor out of municipal tap water so it tastes cleaner and less like a swimming pool. It carries NSF/ANSI 42 certification for that specific claim, and in our blind taste tests it performed on par with pitchers costing twice as much. Where it falls short is scope. This is not a pitcher we'd recommend as a water filter pitcher for lead concerns, because it simply isn't certified or engineered for that job, and the company doesn't publish independent third-party data suggesting otherwise. Treat it as a taste upgrade, not a contaminant-removal device. The filter cartridge is rated for about 40 gallons, which for an average household of two adults translates to roughly a month of daily use before you need a replacement, and those replacements are sold in bulk multi-packs everywhere from the grocery store checkout aisle to the pharmacy counter. Filtration speed is reasonably brisk, filling a standard glass in under 15 seconds once the reservoir has water in it. If your only complaint about your tap water is that it tastes like the municipal treatment plant, this remains a perfectly reasonable, budget-friendly Brita alternative to the name-brand original, or simply a fine choice if you already trust the brand.",
  },
  {
    id: "aquavast-family-xl",
    name: "AquaVast Family XL 18-Cup Pitcher",
    category: "Large-Capacity Activated Carbon",
    badge: "Best for Large Families",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 6100,
    pros: [
      "18-cup reservoir is nearly double the capacity of standard pitchers",
      "Dual-filter design lets you filter and dispense simultaneously",
      "Round-the-clock digital filter life countdown, not a vague sticker reminder",
      "NSF/ANSI 42 and 53 certified, covering taste plus some metals reduction",
      "Spout design fills tall water bottles without repositioning",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Takes up significant refrigerator door or shelf real estate",
      "Heavy when full — awkward for smaller hands to pour one-handed",
      "Two-filter system means double the replacement cost per cycle",
