        <!-- SECTION 1: What They Remove -->
        <div id="what-they-remove" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Do Pitcher Filters Actually Remove?
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the question almost nobody asks before buying, and it's the one that matters most. A water filter pitcher is not a single, standardized product — it's a category that spans everything from basic taste improvement to genuine contaminant removal, and the difference between those two things comes down to filter media and certification, not price or brand recognition alone.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Activated Carbon: The Baseline</h3>
          <p>
            Nearly every pitcher filter on the market uses activated carbon as its primary media, and for good reason — carbon is genuinely effective at adsorbing chlorine, chloramine, and the organic compounds responsible for musty or chemical tastes and odors in tap water. If your only complaint about your water is that it tastes like a public pool, a basic carbon filter will solve that problem, full stop. What activated carbon alone does <strong>not</strong> reliably do is remove dissolved heavy metals like lead, or the persistent synthetic chemicals known as PFAS (per- and polyfluoroalkyl substances). Carbon can trap some of these incidentally, but without additional filter media engineered specifically for that job, results are inconsistent at best.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ion-Exchange Resin: The Metals Layer</h3>
          <p>
            Pitchers that make credible claims about lead or heavy-metal reduction typically add a second layer of ion-exchange resin to the filter cartridge. This media works through a different chemical mechanism than carbon, swapping out target ions like lead for more benign ones as water passes through. It's the component that separates a pitcher genuinely built as a <strong>water filter pitcher for lead</strong> concerns from one that's simply a nicer-tasting carbon filter in a fancier housing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Not All Pitchers Are Equal Here — And That's the Honest Truth</h3>
          <p>
            We'll say this plainly because most buying guides won't: a lot of pitchers marketed with health-adjacent language are only certified for taste and odor. That's not necessarily deceptive — taste and odor reduction is a legitimate, useful thing — but it's frequently misunderstood by shoppers who assume any "filtered" water pitcher is protecting them from contaminants across the board. It isn't. The only reliable way to know what a specific pitcher actually removes is to check its NSF/ANSI certification number against the specific standard, not just look for the phrase NSF certified on the box.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Certification Numbers That Actually Matter</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
              <div>
                <p class="text-2xl font-black text-white">NSF/ANSI 42</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Chlorine taste &amp; odor only — the most common certification, and the least protective.</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-2xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">NSF/ANSI 53</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Health-related contaminants including lead and certain other metals — look for this specifically.</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-2xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">NSF/ANSI 401</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Emerging contaminants, including some PFAS compounds and pharmaceuticals — the most comprehensive tier.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            A <strong>NSF certified water pitcher</strong> claim on packaging is meaningless on its own until you know which of these standards it's certified under. Our top pick in this guide is certified under all three; several perfectly reasonable pitchers we also recommend are only certified under 42 and 53. Neither is wrong — they just serve different needs, and you should know which one you're buying before you check out.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How These Certifications Actually Work</h3>
          <p>
            NSF International is an independent, accredited standards organization, not the manufacturer marketing the pitcher, and that independence is the entire point. A brand cannot simply print NSF/ANSI 53 on a box because it sounds trustworthy — the specific cartridge has to be submitted, tested against a defined contaminant challenge, and re-certified periodically to keep that claim. When we researched every pitcher in this roundup, we looked past the front-of-box language and checked each manufacturer's published certification documentation for the actual standard numbers and the specific contaminants each one covers, because two pitchers can both say "NSF certified" while one is only tested for chlorine taste and the other is tested for lead, mercury, and PFAS. If a pitcher's marketing leans on vague language like advanced filtration or purification technology without citing a specific NSF/ANSI number you can look up, treat that as a signal to dig further before assuming it does more than the basics.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why This Matters More Than the Pitcher's Shape or Color</h3>
          <p>
            It's easy to shop for a water filter pitcher the way you'd shop for a kitchen gadget — comparing capacity, handle comfort, whether it fits your refrigerator door — and treat the filter cartridge itself as an afterthought. We'd encourage the opposite approach. The pitcher housing is largely interchangeable and rarely the differentiator that matters for your health or your water bill over time. The cartridge inside it is doing all the actual work, and that's where your research time is best spent before you swipe a card.
          </p>
        </div>

