        <div id="common-mistakes" class="mt-16 scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            5. Common Water Filter Pitcher Mistakes That Waste Money — Or Make Things Worse
          </h2>

          <p>
            A water filter pitcher is about as simple as home appliances get, but we consistently see the same handful of mistakes undermine what would otherwise be a good purchase. None of these require spending more money to fix — they're almost entirely about how you use and maintain the pitcher you already have, or how carefully you shop before buying one. Here are the four that matter most.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Replacing the Filter on Schedule</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                This is the single most common mistake, and it's also the most consequential one. A filter cartridge that's been used well past its rated gallon capacity doesn't just stop working — it can actively make your water worse than drinking it unfiltered. Once the carbon media is saturated, it loses its ability to bind contaminants, and in some cases the trapped material can begin releasing back into the water passing through. Set an actual reminder based on gallons used, not a vague sense of how many weeks have passed, and replace cartridges on schedule even if the water still looks and tastes fine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming All Pitchers Remove the Same Contaminants</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                We can't stress this enough: the phrase filtered water pitcher covers an enormous range of actual performance. A basic taste-and-odor pitcher and a lead-certified pitcher can look nearly identical on a store shelf while doing fundamentally different jobs. Before buying, check the specific NSF/ANSI certification number rather than trusting general claims like purified or advanced filtration printed on the packaging, which carry no standardized meaning on their own.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Based on Pitcher Capacity Alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A larger reservoir is convenient, but cup capacity and filter quality are entirely separate specifications, and a bigger pitcher tells you nothing about what its filter actually removes or how long it lasts. We've seen shoppers choose an 18-cup pitcher purely for the size, then discover afterward it only carries basic taste-and-odor certification when what they actually needed was lead reduction. Decide what you need filtered out first, then shop for capacity within that group of qualifying products, not the other way around.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the Pitcher Filled Too Long Without Refrigeration</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Filtered water sitting at room temperature for extended periods, particularly once chlorine has been removed from it, creates favorable conditions for bacterial growth. Chlorine's disinfecting effect is exactly what your filter is designed to strip out, which means filtered water loses a layer of protection that unfiltered tap water still has. Keep your pitcher refrigerated, avoid letting filtered water sit for more than a couple of days, and clean the reservoir and lid regularly rather than only rinsing the filter compartment.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

