                The ClearWell Pro Lead Shield Pitcher is our top overall pick because it's NSF/ANSI 53 and 401 certified for lead and PFAS reduction, not just taste and odor like most pitchers. If you want a cheaper everyday option and don't have specific contaminant concerns, the BrightSpring Classic is a solid, widely available choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do water filter pitchers actually remove lead?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only some do, and it depends entirely on certification. A pitcher must be NSF/ANSI 53 certified specifically for lead reduction to make that claim credibly. Many popular pitchers only carry NSF/ANSI 42 certification, which covers chlorine taste and odor but says nothing about lead, heavy metals, or PFAS. Always check the specific certification, not just whether a filter is NSF certified in general.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should you replace a water filter pitcher cartridge?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the filter's rated gallon capacity, which ranges from about 30 gallons on basic budget pitchers to 120 gallons on extended-life models. For an average household of two to three people, that translates to roughly every 2 to 4 weeks for short-life filters and up to 2 to 3 months for long-life filters. Using a filter past its rated capacity can make water quality worse than using no filter at all.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a water filter pitcher enough, or do I need a whole-house system?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A pitcher is fine for improving taste and reducing specific contaminants in drinking water at the tap. If you have whole-house concerns like widespread contamination, well water issues, or want every faucet and shower protected, a whole-house filtration system is the better fit. If your issue is hard water and mineral scale rather than contamination, a water softener addresses a different problem entirely and a pitcher won't help with that at all.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What's the difference between NSF/ANSI 42, 53, and 401 certification?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                NSF/ANSI 42 covers aesthetic effects like chlorine taste and odor reduction only. NSF/ANSI 53 covers health-related contaminants such as lead and certain other metals. NSF/ANSI 401 covers a list of emerging contaminants including some PFAS compounds and pharmaceuticals. A pitcher can carry any combination of these, and only 53 and 401 indicate meaningful health-contaminant reduction beyond taste.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my water filter pitcher so slow?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Slower flow generally means denser filter media and longer contact time between water and the filter, which typically correlates with more thorough contaminant reduction. Fast-flow pitchers use wider funnels and less restrictive media, trading some filtration depth for speed. Neither approach is wrong, but they serve different priorities.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
